"On domination polynomials of some graphs - Combinatorial Press (https://combinatorialpress.com/jcmcc-articles/volume-126/on-domination-polynomials-of-some-graphs/)\nciteturn3view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view0\",\"lineno\":120}); Total lines: 282\nL100: A polynomial \\(p(x)\\) is called symmetric (or self-reciprocal) if \\(\\alpha_i = \\alpha_{n-i}\\) for all \\(0 \\leq i \\leq \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{2} \\right\\rfloor\\). The coefficients of a binomial series are symmetric. Moreover, \\(p(x)\\) is said to be log-concave if it satisfies \\[\\alpha_i^2 \\geq \\alpha_{i-1} \\alpha_{i+1}, \\quad \\text{for all } 1 \\leq i \\leq n-1.\\]\nL101: \nL102: For example, the polynomial \\(1 + 4x + 5x^2 + 51x^3 + 17x^4 + 13x^5\\) is unimodal, whereas \\(41 + 6x + 19x^2 + 6x^3 + 2x^4\\) is not.\nL103: The following classical theorem is very useful in finding the bounds for the zeros of a polynomial.\nL104: Theorem 2.1 (Eneström-Kakeya Theorem [【51†13】]). Let \\(f(x) = \\sum\\limits_{i=0}^{n} a_i x^i\\) be a polynomial with all coefficients \\(a_i > 0\\). Then all the zeros of \\(f(x)\\) lie in the annular region defined by \\[r \\leq |z| \\leq R,\\] where \\[r = \\min \\left\\{ \\left| \\frac{a_i}{a_{i+1}} \\right| : 0 \\leq i \\leq n-1 \\right\\}, \\quad R = \\max \\left\\{ \\left| \\frac{a_i}{a_{i+1}} \\right| : 0 \\leq i \\leq n-1 \\right\\}.\\]\nL105: The friendship (or Dutch-Windmill) graph \\(F_{N}^{2}\\) is a graph obtained by joining vertex of \\(K_{1}\\) with each vertex of \\(n\\) copies of \\(K_{2}.\\) Its order is \\(N=1+2n.\\) The Friendship theorem, (see, Erdös, Rényi, and Sós [【52†10】]) characterizes graphs in which every pair of distinct vertices has precisely one common neighbor, that is, the only graphs satisfying this condition are the friendship graphs. The domination polynomial satisfies the following properties:\nL106: a) \\(D\\Big (\\bigcup_{i=1}^{t}G_{i}, x\\Big ) = \\prod_{i=1}^{t} D(Gi, x).\\)\nL107: \nL108: b) \\(D(G_{1}+ G_{2}, x) = \\big((1 + x)^{n_{1}} -1\\big)\\big((1 + x)^{n_{2}}-1\\big)+ D(G_{1},x)+D(G_{2}, x).\\)\nL109: \nL110: The domination polynomial of friendship graph \\(F_{n}^{2}\\) is given in the following result.\nL111: \nL112: Lemma 2.2. [【35†5】] The domination polynomial of \\(F_{n}^{2}\\) is \\[D(F_{n}^{2}, x) = (2x + x^{2})^{n} + x(1 + x)^{2n}.\\]\nL113: Alikhani, Brown and Jahari [【35†5】] proved the following facts about the domination polynomial of \\(F_{n}^{2}.\\)\nL114: \nL115: a) It is proved that for odd \\(n\\), the polynomial \\(D(F_{n}^{2}, x)\\) has no real non-zero zero for odd \\(n\\).\nL116: \nL117: b) The limit of domination roots of the friendship graphs is \\(-1\\) together with the hyperbola.\nL118: \nL119: c) The friendship \\(F_{n}^{2}\\) and the book graph \\(B_{n}\\) share the same domination polynomial for some vertex deletion of \\(B_{n}.\\)\nL120: \nL121: The following problems were asked in [【35†5】].\nL122: Problem 2.3. For \\(F_{n}^{2}\\) and \\(B_{n}\\), we have the following questions.\nL123: \nL124: a) For \\(n\\) even, does \\(F_{n}^{2}\\) have exactly three real roots?\nL125: \nL126: b) What is a good upper bound on the modulus of the roots of \\(F_{n}\\)?\nL127: \nL128: c) What can be said about the domination roots of book graphs?\nL129: \nL130: Another problem of interest for the domination polynomial of \\(F_{n}^{2}\\) and \\(B_{n}\\) is given as:\nL131: \nL132: Problem 2.4. For \\(F_{n}^{2}\\) and \\(B_{n}\\), we have the following questions.\nL133: a) Are \\(D(F_{n}^{2}, x)\\) and \\(D(B_{n}, x)\\) unimodal and log-concave polynomial?\nL134: \nL135: b) How about the bounds of the zeros of \\(D(F_{n}^{2}, x)\\) and \\(D(B_{n}, x)\\)?\nL136: \nL137: We consider these problems for friendship and book graphs, and discuss their possible answers.\nL138: From Lemma 2.2, we have \\[\\begin{aligned} D(F_{n}^{2}, x) =& (2x + x^{2})^{n} + x(1 + x)^{2n}\\\\ =& \\sum\\limits_{i=0}^{n}\\binom{n}{i}(2x)^{n-i}(x^{2})^{i}+x\\sum\\limits_{i=0}^{2n}\\binom{2n}{i}x^{i}\\\\ =&\\sum\\limits_{i=0}^{n}\\binom{n}{i}2^{n-i}x^{n+i}+\\sum\\limits_{i=0}^{2n}\\binom{2n}{i}x^{i+1}\\\\ =&x+2nx^{2}+\\binom{2n}{2}x^{3}+\\binom{2n}{3}x^{4}+\\dots+\\binom{2n}{n-2}x^{n-1}+x^{n}\\left (2^{n}+\\binom{2n}{n-1}\\right )\\\\ &+x^{n+1}\\left (2^{n-1}\\binom{n}{1}+\\binom{2n}{n}\\right )+x^{n+2}\\left (2^{n-2}\\binom{n}{2}+\\binom{2n}{n+1}\\right )+\\dots+\\\\ &+x^{2n-2}\\left(2^{2}\\binom{n}{n-2}+\\binom{2n}{2n-3} \\right)+x^{2n-1}\\left(2\\binom{n}{n-1}+\\binom{2n}{2n-2} \\right)\\\\ &+x^{2n}(1+2n)+x^{2n+1}.\nL139: \\end{aligned}\\]\nL140: Let \\(a_{i}\\)’s be the coefficients of the above polynomial. Then from the above expression and by the binomial coefficient behaviour, it is clear that \\[a_{1}< a_{2}<\\dots <a_{n+1}> a_{n+2}>a_{n+3}>\\dots>a_{2n-1}>a_{2n}.\\]\nL141: \nL142: Thus, \\(D(F_{n}^{2}, x)\\) is unimodal with peak \\(n+1.\\) Again by the binomial behaviour of coefficients \\(D(F_{n}^{2}, x)\\), the log-concave property follows. Thus, we have the following proposition.\nL143: \nL144: Figure 1 Zeros of \\(D(F_{12}^{2},x)\\) in plane and \\( D(B_{8},x)\\).\nL145: Proposition 2.5. Let \\(D(F_{n}^{2}, x)\\) be the domination polynomial of \\(F_{n}^{2}.\\) Then \\(D(F_{n}^{2}, x)\\) is unimodal and log-concave.\nL146: \nL147: Furthermore, calculating \\(\\frac{a_{i}}{a_{i+1}},\\) for \\(0\\leq i \\leq 2n,\\) we get \\(r>0\\) and \\(R=1+2n.\\) Thus by Eneström-Kakeya Theorem, we have the following consequence.\nL148: \nL149: Proposition 2.6. The zeros of \\(D(F_{n}^{2}, x)\\) lie in the annulus \\[0\\leq |Z|\\leq 1+2n.\\]\nL150: We illustrate Proposition 2.5 and 2.6 by the following example: For \\(n=12,\\) the domination polynomial of \\(F_{12}^{2}\\) is \\[\\begin{aligned} D(F_{12}^{2},x)=&x + 24 x^2 + 276 x^3 + 2024 x^4 + 10626 x^5 + 42504 x^6 + 134596 x^7 + 346104 x^8\\\\ &+ 735471 x^9 + 1307504 x^{10} + 1961256 x^{11} + 2500240 x^{12} + 2728732 x^{13}\\\\ & + 2563728 x^{14} + 2073896 x^{15} + 1434224 x^{16} + 836847 x^{17} + 405240 x^{18} \\\\ &+ 159940 x^{19} + 50424 x^{20} + 12386 x^{21} + 2288 x^{22} + 300 x^{23} + 25 x^{24} + x^{25}.\nL151: \\end{aligned}\\]\nL152: Clearly, \\(D(F_{12}^{2},x)\\) is log-concave and unimodal with peak value \\(n+1=12+1=13\\). Also, the zeros of \\(D(F_{12}^{2},x)\\) lie in \\(0<|Z|<13\\) and their zoomed view is depicted in Figure 1 (left).\nL153: \nL154: For even \\(n\\), the domination polynomial of \\(F_{n}^{2}\\) has exactly three zeros, we are not able to prove, though we verified it computationally for \\(1\\leq n\\leq 100.\\)\nL155: \nL156: From [【35†5】], the domination polynomial of book graphs is given in the following result.\nL157: Proposition 2.7 ([【35†5】]). The domination polynomial of book graph is given as \\[D(B_{n},x)=(2x+x^{2})^{n}(1+2x)+x^{2}(1+x)^{2n}-2x^{n}.\\]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRoot geometry of domination polynomials for friendship and book graphs (https://arxiv.org/html/2604.08998v1)\nciteturn3view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view1\",\"lineno\":60}); Total lines: 515\nL53: The results are intresting in two specific respects. Initially, they resolve a previously unresolved precise enumeration issue concerning the real roots of friendship graphs. Secondly, they transform the qualitative root plots of [【29†7】] into quantitative theorems: precise root counts, monotonic convergence, a sublinear modulus bound, and limit characterization for the zeros of book graphs.\nL54: The combination of analytic inequalities, recurrence-free algebraic transformations, and asymptotic root-limit techniques is particularly efficacious for domination polynomials of dense graph families exhibiting repetitive local structures.\nL55: The article is organized as: Section 【5†2】 compiles the notation, fundamental identities, and established results utilized throughout the article.\nL56: Section 【6†3】 provides a comprehensive resolution to Question 3.1 [【29†7】] by demonstrating that even friendship graphs possess precisely three real domination roots and by accurately identifying their two nonzero roots. Section 【7†4】 addresses Question 3.2 [【29†7】] by formulating a novel global modulus estimate for the complex roots of $D(F_{n},x)$ and providing a clear sublinear upper bound.\nL57: Section 【8†5】 addresses Question 3.3 [【29†7】]: we ascertain the  limit set for the roots of the book graphs and infer multiple implications regarding real roots. Section 【9†6】 pertains to Question 3.6 [【29†7】]. We show that friendship graphs and book graphs do not possess nonzero integer domination roots and clarify how this relates to known corona structures, identifying $-2$ as the only nonzero integer root in the previously understood families.\nL58: Section 【10†7】 concludes the paper by presenting the conclusions, limitations, and possibilities for future research.\nL59: ## 2 Preliminaries and notation\nL60: \nL61: We write $N(v)$ and $N[v]$ for the open and closed neighborhoods of a vertex $v$, and for a subset $S\\subseteq V(G)$ we use $N[S]=\\bigcup_{v\\in S}N[v]$. A dominating set is a set $S$ with $N[S]=V(G)$. The set of distinct zeros of $D(G,x)$ is denoted by $Z(D(G,x))$.\nL62: For $n\\geq 1$, the friendship graph $F_{n}$ is the graph obtained by gluing $n$ triangles at a common vertex, see Figure 【35†1】 (a). Equivalently, $F_{n}=K_{1}+nK_{2},$ where $+$ is join operation (obtained from two graphs by joining each vertex of first graph with every vertex of second graph). For $n\\geq 1$, the book graph $B_{n}$ is formed by identifying a common edge in $n$ copies of $C_{4}$, see Figure 【35†1】 (b).\nL63: \nL64: Figure 1: A friendship $F_{3}$, and a book graph $B_{4}$.\nL65: We now record the known formulas needed later.\nL66: \nL67: The following result gives the domination polynomial formula for union and join of graphs.\nL68: ###### Proposition 2.1 ([【36†2】, 【37†18】]).\nL69: \nL70: If $G_{1}$ and $G_{2}$ are graphs of orders $n_{1}$ and $n_{2}$, then\nL71: \nL72:  | $$D(G_{1}\\cup G_{2},x)=D(G_{1},x)D(G_{2},x),$$  |\nL73: \nL74: and\nL75: \nL76:  | $$D(G_{1}+G_{2},x)=\\bigl((1+x)^{n_{1}}-1\\bigr)\\bigl((1+x)^{n_{2}}-1\\bigr)+D(G_{1},x)+D(G_{2},x).$$  |\nL77: \nL78: Next result gives the known formula for the domination polynomial of friendship graphs.\nL79: ###### Theorem 2.2 ([【29†7】]).\nL80: \nL81: For every $n\\geq 1$,\nL82: \nL83:  | $$D(F_{n},x)=(2x+x^{2})^{n}+x(1+x)^{2n}.$$  |\nL84: \nL85: The following formula gives the domination polynomial for book graphs.\nL86: \nL87: ###### Theorem 2.3 ([【29†7】]).\nL88: \nL89: For every $n\\geq 1$,\nL90: \nL91:  | $$D(B_{n},x)=(x^{2}+2x)^{n}(2x+1)+x^{2}(1+x)^{2n}-2x^{n}.$$  |\nL92: ###### Definition 2.4 (Limit of roots).\nL93: \nL94: Let $\\{f_{n}(x)\\}$ be a sequence of polynomials. A complex number $z$ is called a limit of roots if either $f_{n}(z)=0$ for all sufficiently large $n$, or $z$ is a limit point of the union of all root sets $Z(f_{n})$.\nL95: \nL96: The following theorem gives the alternative idea for finding the limit of the zeros of a polynomial.\nL97: ###### Theorem 2.5 (Beraha–Kahane–Weiss, restated from [【34†9】, 【17†12】]).\nL98: \nL99: Suppose\nL100: \nL101:  | $$f_{n}(x)=\\alpha_{1}(x)\\lambda_{1}(x)^{n}+\\cdots+\\alpha_{k}(x)\\lambda_{k}(x)^{n},$$  |\nL102: \nL103: where the $\\alpha_{j}$ and $\\lambda_{j}$ are fixed nonzero polynomials and no pair $\\lambda_{i},\\lambda_{j}$ differs by a unimodular constant factor identically. Then $z$ is a limit of roots of ${f_{n}}$ if and only if either:\nL104: \nL105:   1. (i)\nL106: at least two of the numbers $|\\lambda_{j}(z)|$ are equal and strictly larger than all the others, or\nL107: \nL108:   2. (ii)\nL109: \nL110: for some $j$, the modulus $|\\lambda_{j}(z)|$ is strictly larger than all others and $\\alpha_{j}(z)=0$.\nL111: \nL112: The following problems were asked in [【29†7】].\nL113: ###### Open Problem 2.6.\nL114: \nL115: The following problems about the domination polynomial are open.\nL116: \nL117:   1. (i)\nL118: \nL119: (Question 3.1 [【29†7】]) For $n$ even, does $F_{n}$ have exactly three real roots?\nL120: \nL121:   2. (ii)\nL122: \nL123: (Question 3.2 [【29†7】]) What is a good upper bound on the modulus of the roots of $F_{n}$?\nL124: \nL125:   3. (iii)\nL126: \nL127: (Question 3.3 [【29†7】]) What can be said about the domination roots of book graphs?\nL128: \nL129:   4. (iv)\nL130: \nL131: (Question 3.6 [【29†7】]) Is $-2$ the only possible nonzero integer domination root?\nL132: The paper [【29†7】] showed that when $n$ is odd the friendship graph $F_{n}$ has no nonzero real domination root, and when $n$ is even it has at least two nonzero real roots in $(-2,0)$. It also identified the limiting hyperbola for the complex roots of $F_{n}$ and derived the explicit formula for $D(B_{n},x)$. However, the exact number of real roots for even $n$, a usable upper bound on the modulus of domination roots of $F_{n}$, and a systematic root-limit theorem for the book graphs were not supplied.\nL133: The integer-root problem also remained open in general. These are precisely the gaps addressed in the present paper.\nL134: ## 3 Question 3.1: Exact number of real roots of $F_{n}$\nL135: \nL136: This section gives the exact real-root count for friendship graphs when $n$ is even.\nL137: \nL138: The following result gives the affirmative answer Question 3.1 from [【29†7】].\nL139: ###### Theorem 3.1.\nL140: \nL141: If $n$ is even, then $D(F_{n},x)$ has exactly three real zeros, namely $0$ and two simple zeros\nL142: \nL143:  | $$x_{n}^{-}\\in(-2,-1),\\qquad x_{n}^{+}\\in(-1,0).$$  |\nL144: \nL145: Proof. By Theorem 【38†2.2】, the domination polynomial of friendship graph is\nL146: \nL147:  | $$D(F_{n},x)=x^{n}(x+2)^{n}+x(1+x)^{2n}.$$  |\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://ijmex.com/index.php/ijmex/article/download/419/283)\ncite